    <h1>How to Specify and Source Dried Chilli for Food Manufacturing</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A dried chilli specification is more than a heat level. This guide lays out the full chain — from
      variety and origin, through form, mesh and moisture, to food safety, packaging and supplier
      qualification — so you can write a spec that produces the same product every batch.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Specify the variety (or SHU window), origin, form, mesh, moisture,
      water activity, SHU, ASTA colour and the food-safety parameters for your market. Then verify with
      a lot COA and a qualified supplier. Heat, colour, texture, safety and shelf life are each controlled
      by a different number — miss one and the batch changes.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The core things to specify</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Variety (or SHU window)</strong> — "Sanying, 20,000–50,000 SHU", not "hot chilli".</li>
      <li><strong>Origin / growing region</strong> — variety performance varies by region.</li>
      <li><strong>Form</strong> — whole, flakes, segments or powder.</li>
      <li><strong>Mesh size</strong> — 60–80 standard powder; 20–40 flakes; 100+ smooth sauces.</li>
      <li><strong>Moisture and water activity</strong> — under ~13% moisture, low a<sub>w</sub>.</li>
      <li><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong> and <strong>colour (ASTA)</strong> — see the dedicated guides.</li>
      <li><strong>Food-safety parameters</strong> — microbial, pesticide, mycotoxin, heavy metals.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why "chilli powder" is not a specification</h2>
    <p>
      Heat, colour and flavour vary independently between varieties. Two powders can both be "chilli
      powder" yet sit 40,000 SHU apart. The variety, origin and the SHU window are what pin the product
      down — and they fix the trade-off you cannot otherwise control.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Origin and growing region</h2>
    <p>
      The same variety grown in different regions can differ in heat, colour and size — water stress and
      climate shift capsaicinoid and pigment production. Naming the region (for example, Sichuan for
      Erjingtiao, Gansu for Jinta) is part of the specification, not a footnote. It is also what lets a
      supplier hold a batch consistent crop to crop.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Form: whole, flakes, segments or powder</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Whole pods</strong> — longest shelf life, slowest colour/aroma loss; used for display,
      grinding at destination, or rehydration.</li>
      <li><strong>Flakes / crushed (20–40 mesh)</strong> — visible texture; retain aroma better than fine
      powder.</li>
      <li><strong>Segments</strong> — mid-size pieces for sauces and blends.</li>
      <li><strong>Powder (60–80 mesh)</strong> — even dispersion and smooth mouthfeel, but faster colour
      and aroma fade due to surface area.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Form is a <em>flavour and shelf-life decision</em>, not just texture — a fine powder oxidises
      faster than a whole pod, so choose form against your lead time and storage.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Mesh and moisture — the two people forget</h2>
    <p>
      Mesh controls particle size and how evenly heat and colour disperse. Moisture (under ~13%) controls
      shelf life and clumping. <strong>Water activity</strong> is the better safety indicator than
      moisture alone — a product can read acceptable moisture yet still have high free water. Both are
      cheap to get right and expensive to get wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Heat, colour and the independent dimensions</h2>
    <p>
      Heat (<a href="/guides/asta-colour-and-shu-heat-explained/">SHU</a>), colour
      (<a href="/guides/asta-colour-value/">ASTA</a>) and flavour are three independent dimensions. A
      complete spec names the heat window, the colour window where colour matters, and the variety that
      ties them together. See the linked guides for how each is measured and why each varies.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Food-safety parameters</h2>
    <p>
      A spec that ignores food safety will fail at the border. State the
      <a href="/guides/food-safety-quality/">mycotoxin, pesticide, microbial and heavy-metal
      limits</a> for your target market — the EU, for example, caps aflatoxin B1 at 5 µg/kg and total
      aflatoxins at 10 µg/kg for dried chilli. These limits differ by market, so the destination belongs
      in the specification.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Packaging, shelf life and storage</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Packaging</strong> — kraft bags, cartons or vacuum; moisture-barrier for long transit.</li>
      <li><strong>Shelf life</strong> — typically 18–24 months under correct storage; colour fades with
      oxygen, light and heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Storage</strong> — cool, dark, dry, sealed; storage is part of the spec because it is
      where colour and aroma are lost.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">A worked example specification</h2>
    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Example:</strong> "Sanying chilli powder, Shandong origin, 20,000–50,000 SHU, 60–80 mesh,
      moisture ≤13%, a<sub>w</sub> ≤0.65, ASTA ≥100, EU aflatoxin limits, packed 25 kg kraft bags, export
      grade."
    </div>
    <p>
      Everything is pinned: variety, origin, heat, texture, moisture, safety, colour and packaging. A
      supplier who sees this can quote accurately; a supplier who sees only "chilli powder" has to guess
      — and a guess is a batch that changes every order.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Batch consistency, COA and sampling</h2>
    <p>
      A specification is only as good as the verification. Request a <strong>lot-specific COA</strong>
      — a certificate of analysis showing the <em>measured</em> SHU, ASTA, moisture, microbial and
      mycotoxin values for the lot you are buying, not a range copied from a catalogue. Agree a sampling
      protocol and retain samples; this is what turns a written spec into repeatable product.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Supplier qualification and factory audit</h2>
    <p>
      Qualify the supplier before the order: ask for food-safety certification (HACCP, ISO 22000 or
      equivalent), traceability from growing base to finished lot, and — for large programmes — a factory
      audit. A supplier who can show the production chain and the lot paperwork is the difference between
      a source and a risk. See our <a href="/quality-certifications/">quality &amp; certifications</a>
      page for what a real food-safety control chain looks like.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Shipping, documentation and import</h2>
    <p>
      Chilli ships by <a href="/guides/sea-vs-air-freight/">sea or air</a>; the choice is a lead-time
      versus cost trade-off. Prepare the import documentation up front — commercial invoice, packing list,
      COA, phytosanitary where required, and any market-specific certificates. Confirm the destination's
      contaminant limits before you book, not after the shipment is held.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">OEM and private label</h2>
    <p>
      If you sell under your own brand, specify the <strong>packaging, grind, blend and label</strong> as
      part of the order. OEM/ODM lets you fix a private recipe and packaging format; the same specification
      discipline applies — the difference is that you now own the brand on top of the spec.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The three mistakes that change your batch</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Specifying "hot" or "mild"</strong> — no SHU window means the supplier picks a variety, and it will drift.</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting moisture and water activity</strong> — invisible at quotation time, they ruin the batch later.</li>
      <li><strong>Specifying heat and colour but not variety or origin</strong> — you cannot optimise all three independently; the variety and region set the trade-off.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The Author's Take</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Position:</strong> In my view, most chilli sourcing failures are specification failures —
      the buyer names a heat level and leaves the other six parameters to guesswork.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reasoning:</strong> Heat, colour, texture, safety and shelf life each drift on their own
      when unspecified. A one-line spec that names variety, origin, form, mesh, moisture, SHU, ASTA and
      the market's food-safety limits costs nothing to write and saves a failed batch. The buyers who get
      consistent product are the ones who specify all of it and then verify with a lot COA.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as a dried-chilli supplier's technical team,
      not a contractual or regulatory statement. The example values are representative ranges — confirm
      your exact SHU, ASTA, moisture and market limits with us before contracting.
    </p>

